<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">

  <!--
    Zero-Trust CSP (DAI v0.1, spec §1) as widened for the Phase 2 iframe runtime.

    `connect-src 'none'` is the air gap and is untouched: no fetch, XHR,
    WebSocket, EventSource or beacon can leave this document. Every other
    directive names only `blob:` — origin-local object URLs minted from the
    embedded payload, which cannot reach the network — plus the inline/wasm
    allowances the React bundle and sqlite3.wasm require.

    `blob:` on script-src/style-src and `frame-src 'self' blob:` are additions
    over the v0.1 spec text: with `default-src 'none'` the bootloader could
    neither frame the app nor load a single packaged asset. Do not add any
    scheme that can reach a remote host.
  -->
  <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="default-src 'none'; script-src 'nonce-9069aa640da72f1e3414dff07c90cec9' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' blob:; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' data: blob:; frame-src 'self' blob:; worker-src blob:; connect-src 'none'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none';">

  <!--
    The channels connect-src does not govern, closed where a page can close
    them. DNS prefetch is switched off here and in the application's frame.
    Speculation rules need 'inline-speculation-rules' or a nonce, and the
    policy above grants the first to nothing, while the runtime never stamps
    its nonce on a speculation rules script. WebRTC stays open: no policy a
    page can set closes it in a browser host.
  -->
  <meta http-equiv="x-dns-prefetch-control" content="off">

  <!--
    viewport-fit=cover lets the app paint under the notch and home indicator;
    without it a standalone iOS launch is letterboxed by safe-area insets.
  -->
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">

  <!--
    iOS standalone support, so a container served over HTTPS is Add to Home
    Screen ready with no extra work. These are inert when opened from file://
    and cost nothing there.

    apple-mobile-web-app-capable is the legacy spelling iOS still requires;
    mobile-web-app-capable is the standard one other engines read.
  -->
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
  <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent">
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="DAI Demo App">
  <meta name="application-name" content="DAI Demo App">
  <title>DAI Demo App</title>

  <!-- Favicon and Apple Touch Icon (supports custom data URL or SVG default) -->
  <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22 viewBox=%220 0 100 100%22%3E%3Crect width=%22100%22 height=%22100%22 rx=%2220%22 fill=%22%230f172a%22/%3E%3Cpath d=%22M30 25 L70 25 L70 40 L45 40 L45 60 L70 60 L70 75 L30 75 Z%22 fill=%22%233b82f6%22/%3E%3Ccircle cx=%2275%22 cy=%2270%22 r=%228%22 fill=%22%2310b981%22/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22 viewBox=%220 0 100 100%22%3E%3Crect width=%22100%22 height=%22100%22 rx=%2220%22 fill=%22%230f172a%22/%3E%3Cpath d=%22M30 25 L70 25 L70 40 L45 40 L45 60 L70 60 L70 75 L30 75 Z%22 fill=%22%233b82f6%22/%3E%3Ccircle cx=%2275%22 cy=%2270%22 r=%228%22 fill=%22%2310b981%22/%3E%3C/svg%3E">

  <!--
    Integrity policy, fixed at compile time and stored in the shell rather than
    in the payload. runtime/manifest.json cannot switch verification off: a
    manifest that could disable its own enforcement is not an integrity control.
  -->
  <meta name="dai-integrity" content="required">

  <!--
    Publisher's public key (base64 SPKI), empty when the container is unsigned.
    From manifest 3 the shell is outside the signed set, checked against its
    own digest and the live document; the key sits here because a key inside
    the signed set could not be written before signing.
  -->
  <meta name="dai-public-key" content="">

  <style>
    :root { color-scheme: light dark; }
    html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; }
    /*
     * Out of the top-right corner.
     *
     * The host floats its own menu there now, over the application, the way a
     * phone floats what little chrome it needs. Two round controls in one
     * corner is one too many, and this is the one somebody reaches for least.
     */
    #dai-app-mode { bottom: max(10px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom)); }
    body {
      font: 14px/1.5 ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
      background: Canvas;
      color: CanvasText;
    }
    /*
     * Behind a mounted application: its own colour, when the host has said
     * what that is (--dai-ground, from the host's measurement of the app).
     * Pulling the app down past its top on a phone shows what is behind it,
     * and that was this shell's white over an app of any other colour.
     */
    body.dai-mounted, body.dai-mounted #dai-app { background: var(--dai-ground, Canvas); }
    #boot-ui {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      height: 100%;
      padding: 2rem;
      text-align: center;
    }
    #boot-ui code { opacity: 0.7; word-break: break-word; }
    /*
      App Mode control. It lives in the top document because only the top
      document can request fullscreen — a sandboxed frame cannot, and the app
      should not have to ask for the capability to be forwarded.
    */
    #dai-app-mode {
      position: fixed;
      bottom: 10px;
      right: max(10px, env(safe-area-inset-right));
      z-index: 2147483647;
      padding: 6px 12px;
      font: inherit;
      font-size: 12px;
      line-height: 1;
      color: CanvasText;
      background: Canvas;
      border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 25%, transparent);
      border-radius: 6px;
      opacity: 0.35;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: opacity 0.15s ease;
    }
    #dai-app-mode:hover,
    #dai-app-mode:focus-visible { opacity: 1; }
    /* Hidden until the app reports in. */
    body:not(.dai-mounted) #dai-app-mode { display: none; }
    /*
      Deliberately still shown in App Mode, just fainter. Escape is not a
      discoverable exit, and hiding the only control would strand anyone who
      does not know it.
    */
    body.dai-app-mode #dai-app-mode { opacity: 0.12; }

    #dai-app {
      position: fixed;
      inset: 0;
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
      border: 0;
      background: Canvas;
    }
    /* The mounted application covers the boot UI only once it has reported in. */
    body:not(.dai-mounted) #dai-app { visibility: hidden; }
    body.dai-mounted #boot-ui { display: none; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="boot-ui">
    <div>
      <p><strong id="dai-app-name"></strong></p>
      <!--
        Written to be true if nothing else ever happens.

        The bootloader replaces this the moment it runs. When it cannot run —
        blocked by a viewer, or in a preview that renders a document without
        executing it — this line is the only thing anybody will ever see, and a
        word like "Opening" leaves them watching a file that is not opening.
        A message that is a spinner when things work and an explanation when
        they do not costs nothing and is the difference between a stall and an
        answer.
      -->
      <p id="dai-boot-status">
        Opening&hellip; if this does not change, this viewer only shows the
        file and cannot run it. On a phone: save it to Files, then go to
        opendai.app and choose it. On a computer: open it in a browser.
      </p>
      <p><code id="dai-boot-detail"></code></p>
      <noscript>
        <!--
          A preview that renders the document without running it leaves the
          line above on screen forever, describing work that never started.
          This is the only message that can reach somebody in that situation,
          because everything else here needs the script that is not running.
        -->
        <p>
          This container needs scripting, and this viewer has it switched off.
          Nothing is wrong with the file. Open it in a browser, or with the DAI
          runner on a phone.
        </p>
      </noscript>
    </div>
  </div>

  <button id="dai-app-mode" type="button" hidden>Enter App Mode</button>

  <!-- The Viewer/Bootloader Engine (bundled from src/runtime/bootloader.ts). -->
